---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "../config/site";
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="What TimeAtlas is, how it works without any paid APIs, and exactly where its city, time zone, and map data comes from."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-paper-100">About TimeAtlas</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-paper-400">Explore the world. Find your time.</p>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">What this is</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        TimeAtlas is a world clock: search a city or country, see its local time next to
        yours, compare several at once, and look them up on an interactive globe. It's built
        for the practical problem of comparing time zones and planning calls across them —
        the globe is there to make that understandable and a little more engaging, not as a
        novelty on its own.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Almost everything runs in your browser. Search, clocks, time-zone math, comparisons,
        and your saved preferences all work entirely client-side, using
        your device's own clock and the time zone database built into your browser. There is
        no user account system and no paid third-party API behind any of this.
      </p>
    </section>

    {siteConfig.operatorName && (
      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Who runs this</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
          TimeAtlas is operated by {siteConfig.operatorName}.
        </p>
      </section>
    )}

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">What it depends on, honestly</h2>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-paper-300">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">Your device's clock.</strong> The "Live" time shown
          anywhere on TimeAtlas is read directly from your device's system clock. It is not
          checked against an atomic clock, a GPS signal, or any time server. If your device's
          clock is wrong, so is the time you see here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">Your browser's time zone database.</strong> Offsets
          and daylight-saving rules come from the IANA time zone database as implemented by
          your browser (via the Intl JavaScript API). Rule changes take effect once your
          browser updates that database — see the <a href="/guide/#accuracy" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">accuracy notes</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">A finite city dataset.</strong> TimeAtlas's search
          covers a large but bounded set of cities — see <a href="/guide/#coverage" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">coverage notes</a> for exactly what's included.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">A simplified globe.</strong> The 3D globe is a
          stylized model with approximate day/night shading, not satellite imagery or a live
          feed — see <a href="/guide/#globe" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">what the globe does and doesn't show</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">What "no paid APIs" does and doesn't mean</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        TimeAtlas doesn't call any paid or metered third-party API, and doesn't require you —
        or us — to hold an API key for it to work. That's a statement about the software, not
        about the cost of running it: serving this site still involves ordinary, real-world
        costs like web hosting, bandwidth, and (for the public production domain) domain
        registration. None of that is free merely because no API bill is attached to it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="data-sources" class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Data sources and licensing</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Full version numbers, license text, and attribution requirements for every dataset and
        asset are recorded in <code class="tabular">DATA_SOURCES.md</code> in the project
        repository. In short:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-paper-300">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">Cities, regions, and time zones:</strong>
          <a href="https://www.geonames.org/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GeoNames.org</a>,
          licensed under
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">CC BY 4.0</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">Country/time-zone relationships:</strong> the IANA
          time zone database (public domain).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">Country boundaries:</strong>
          <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Natural Earth</a>
          (public domain), via the MIT-licensed
          <a href="https://github.com/datasets/geo-countries" class="underline hover:text-paper-100" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">datasets/geo-countries</a>
          GeoJSON packaging.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-paper-100">Earth textures:</strong> NASA Visible Earth (Blue
          Marble) and NASA Earth Observatory (Black Marble) imagery, both public domain,
          resized and re-compressed for the web.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Natural Earth's boundary data documents its own conventions for disputed and de facto
        borders; TimeAtlas draws them as published and doesn't intend this as a political
        endorsement of any claim.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
